    {
      "id": "UgxPGO0_3FzSwX6vG0Z4AaABAg",
      "author": "@PomPomsuper",
      "authorImage": "https://yt3.ggpht.com/OKVnB4kQ7QJlymvcMp-fCEUlxpPqFjJbWSas6d5ke_Q-TvNKTsQZ8qI0xn6PV6WPFhlupoRcNA=s48-c-k-c0x00ffffff-no-rj",
      "text": "In this town, there's a hotel where every room is a different year, room 1985, room 1776, room 2090, and you can only check in once in your whole life. That's why people here save their stay for when they're old. Historians wait in line for years. Couples go back to the night they first met. Anyone who visits the future has to sign a contract promising never to tell what they saw. And the man at the front desk is over three hundred years old. He says he's seen everything twice. My dad is the quietest man I know. He goes to work, comes home, and barely talks. He only smiles when he tells one story: his best friend from when he was thirteen, a kid named Noah, same name as me. They spent one whole summer together, riding bikes and building a treehouse. And then, on the last day of summer, Noah just vanished. No goodbye, no address, nothing. My dad says he never had a friend like that again. So on my thirteenth birthday, I walked into the hotel, put on my favorite red hoodie, and asked for room one nine nine nine, the year my dad was thirteen. The old man at the desk handed me a golden key and said, one summer, then the bell rings and you come back. And remember, kid, you can't tell anyone who you are. I opened the door and the air smelled different, old cars, big phones. And there, at the end of the street, was a skinny kid on a bike with a broken chain. It was my dad, thirteen years old, with the same goofy grin he has in all his old photos. I helped him fix the chain. He asked my name, and I panicked and said the first thing that came to my mind: Noah. He grinned. Cool, I'm Jake. That summer was the best summer of my life. We rode bikes until it got dark. We built a treehouse with his dad's old wood. We ate frozen pizza and stayed up watching scary movies. He taught me how to skip rocks on the lake. My dad, the quietest man I know, never stopped talking. He told terrible jokes. He laughed so hard he snorted. I had never seen him like that. And slowly, I started to understand. Wait, a kid named Noah, one perfect summer, a treehouse, a kid who disappeared on the last day without saying goodbye. The best friend my dad lost, the one he named me after, was me. It had always been me. Every story he ever told me suddenly made sense. And on the last day of summer, I heard it: a bell ringing somewhere far away. My time was up. Jake was sitting in the treehouse, holding an old camera. One picture, he said, so I don't forget you. We squeezed together and the flash went off. Then I climbed down the ladder. Where are you going? he yelled. I wanted to tell him everything, that he would grow up, that he would have a son, that the son would be me, but I couldn't. I had promised. So I just looked back and said, I'll see you again, Jake. When? he asked. I smiled. Sooner than you think. And I walked through the door. When I got home, the house was quiet. The garage light was on. My dad was sitting on the floor surrounded by old boxes, holding a faded photo in his hands, two kids in a treehouse. One of them was thirteen-year-old Jake. The other one was wearing a red hoodie, my red hoodie. My dad looked at the photo, then at me, then at the photo again. His hands were shaking. And for the first time in my whole life, I saw my dad do something I had only seen in 1999. He laughed, that same goofy snorting laugh. Noah? he whispered. I sat down next to him on the cold garage floor. Told you, I said, sooner than you think.",
      "likeCount": 0,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-07T23:08:47Z",
      "replyCount": 0,
      "replies": []
    },
